p — so the operative test is the single condition and a reader treating the parenthesis as a second independent path over-rates the conjunct. P(>=25% MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown, 2027-2036) = 0.74 (base rate ~6-8 episodes in 53-55 years = 65-70% per decade, lifted for the $1.4T circular vendor/cloud/chip commitments on ~$300B equity and ~$650B debt, index concentration, and AI-Q4 at 0.66). P(hedge fails | drawdown) decomposed by SHOCK TYPE, which is where the arithmetic bites: AI-capex bust / demand shock 0.48 weight x 0.12 fail = 0.058; inflation / supply / fiscal-dominance shock 0.26 x 0.80 = 0.208; duration indigestion / failed auction / convenience-yield or basis-trade unwind 0.16 x 0.85 = 0.136; geopolitical 0.10 x 0.40 = 0.040. Sum = 0.442, plus 0.03 for an aggregation feature the panel missed — the test is on G7 GDP-WEIGHTED nominals, so a US flight-to-quality rally can be offset by a JGB or BTP selloff, making the hedge more likely to \"fail\" on the published measure than on a US-only read. P(fail|drawdown) = 0.47. P(B) = 0.74 x 0.47 = 0.348. The self-cancelling structure is real and I preserve it: the single most likely cause of a 25% drawdown here is the AI capex bust, which is disinflationary and makes bonds rally, i.e. the most probable drawdown is the one that FAILS the second clause.\nCORRELATION: rho = +0.45, the same fiscal-dominance and fragile-plumbing regime generates both legs.\nJOINT = 0.44x0.348 + 0.45xsqrt(0.44x0.56x0.348x0.652) = 0.153 + 0.45x0.2365 = 0.153+0.106 = 0.259 -> 26. Frechet check: 0.259 <= min(0.44, 0.348) = 0.348, feasible.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.780Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"G7 GDP-weighted 10y real yield (PPP-weighted, computed on the same seven benchmarks) averages below 1.00% for four consecutive quarters, with at least one G7 sover